{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"The transfer experiment completed on a clean copy (CheckRun 2b61659461634c04b53ec61c35541227, exit 0) and answered the open question with a mix stated precisely: the named target really held no vote (target_suffrage=Nonvoter), the caller's future reported no error, yet the named target never reached Leader in the window and ended as Follower, while the cluster term moved from 2 to 4 and the only server seen leading was the original leader, in Leader state at the end. The request was therefore accepted and reported as done while the authority it names did not move to that server. The impossibility is source-fixed rather than window-dependent: a vote-less member gets no self-vote in electSelf (a vote is cast only for servers whose suffrage is Voter), voters reject its RequestVote after adopting its higher term, and only voters start elections; the explicit path accepted it because it only looks for a replication state, whereas the implicit pickServer path deliberately skips non-voters, and the caller sees no error because losing leadership during a transfer is answered with nil. Reading the run's own retained DEBUG log turned the reconstructed sequence into an observed one: the target enters candidate state at term=3 with no pre-vote success line and asks all three voters for a vote while never logging a self-vote, each voter logs term adoption immediately followed by rejecting the vote request since that node is not a voter, the original leader then campaigns at term=4 with a pre-vote tally of 2 of votesNeeded 2 and re-enters leader state, and the same log names the lost-leadership branch for the caller's future. I recorded the witnessed fact, answered the gap, asserted the scoped defect with its conditions, and kept three counterweights and three limits, including the absence of a same-window control for the term movement; the newly queued control runs the identical transfer path with a voter as the target and is tagged as a separate event so the two cases stay distinguishable. Earlier witnessed findings on the configuration index and on duties lost at shutdown remain recorded, and this turn adds nothing beyond what the receipt and its log support.\"}"}}
